  <p>
    Hair loss in women usually presents differently from men: diffuse thinning over the top of the
    scalp with a widening parting, rather than a receding hairline. Complete baldness is
    uncommon; what changes is density.
  </p>

  <p>
    The crucial difference in how it should be managed is that
    <strong>correctable causes are common.</strong> Iron deficiency, thyroid disease and PCOS
    account for a substantial share, and all three are treatable. This is why a consultation that
    goes straight to a serum without investigating is doing it backwards.
  </p>

  <h2>The main causes</h2>

  <h3>Female pattern hair loss</h3>
  <p>
    Gradual thinning over the crown, with the parting widening over years. Genetic, and often
    becoming apparent around the menopause. Managed rather than cured.
  </p>

  <h3>Telogen effluvium</h3>
  <p>
    Sudden, dramatic, diffuse shedding — handfuls in the shower — starting two to three months
    after a trigger: illness, high fever, surgery, childbirth, rapid weight loss, severe stress,
    or starting certain medicines. It recovers on its own. Recognising it saves months of
    unnecessary treatment.
  </p>

  <h3>Nutritional and hormonal</h3>
  <p>
    Iron deficiency is the most common and is frequently missed because haemoglobin can be normal
    while ferritin is low. Thyroid disease, vitamin D deficiency and PCOS all contribute.
  </p>

  <h3>Traction alopecia</h3>
  <p>
    From sustained tension — tight plaits, buns, extensions. It recedes at the temples and
    hairline, and if the tension continues long enough the loss becomes permanent.
  </p>

  <h3>Scarring alopecia</h3>
  <p>
    Frontal fibrosing alopecia and lichen planopilaris destroy follicles permanently. They are
    less common but genuinely urgent, because treatment can only preserve what has not yet been
    lost. A scalp examination identifies them; a conversation about shampoo does not.
  </p>

  <h2>How it is assessed</h2>

  <p>
    History first: the pattern, whether it is shedding or thinning, recent illness or delivery,
    cycle regularity, diet, medicines, and hairstyling. Then examination of the scalp, with
    trichoscopy to look at follicle calibre and spacing and to identify scarring if present.
  </p>

  <p>
    Blood tests almost always: ferritin, thyroid function, vitamin D, and a hormonal profile where
    PCOS is suspected — irregular cycles, acne along the jawline, excess facial hair.
  </p>

  <h2>Treatment</h2>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Correct what is correctable</strong> — iron, thyroid, vitamin D. This alone resolves a meaningful proportion of cases</li>
    <li><strong>Topical minoxidil</strong> — the main evidence-based treatment for pattern hair loss. Expect early shedding and continue through it</li>
    <li><strong>Anti-androgens</strong> — spironolactone, or oral contraceptives in PCOS-related loss, where appropriate</li>
    <li><strong>PRP and GFC</strong> — useful as an addition to medical treatment, particularly for diffuse thinning</li>
    <li><strong>Change the hairstyling</strong> — in traction alopecia this is the treatment, and no medicine substitutes for it</li>
    <li><strong>Prompt treatment for scarring alopecia</strong> — to preserve remaining follicles</li>
  </ul>

  <div class="note">
    <h3>About the shedding that follows starting treatment</h3>
    <p>
      Minoxidil pushes resting follicles into a new growth cycle, and the old hairs are shed first.
      Shedding at weeks two to six is expected and it means the treatment is working. Almost
      everyone who stops at this point concludes it made things worse.
    </p>
  </div>
